{{Japanese name|Koyanagi}}

'''[[Ryuichi Koyanagi]]''' ({{nihongo|小柳 龍一|Koyanagi Ryūichi}}; 9 March 1841 – 15 October 1922) was a Sanese politician who served as the 5th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for two non-consecutive terms: first from March to September 1898, and again from 1911 to 1913. He led the first party-based cabinet in Okaiken's history, though his Kenseitō government collapsed after only six months due to internal divisions between its constituent factions. His second term championed the Go-Shōkō Democracy movement and civilian control of the government before collapsing during a political crisis with the Army.

==Early life and education==
[[Ryuichi Koyanagi]] was born in [[Iwate Prefecture|Iwate]] in 1841, the son of a wealthy merchant family that had profited from the opening of the northern ports to foreign trade during the final years of the [[Honda Shogunate]]. Unlike many of the genrō who came from samurai backgrounds, Koyanagi was a commoner—a fact that would shape his political identity and his commitment to democratic reform.

Koyanagi attended the commercial academy in Iwate before moving to [[Okami Imperial University]], where he studied law and political economy. He was deeply influenced by the Volisanian liberal tradition and became an early advocate for constitutional government and party-based democracy.

==Political career==
Koyanagi entered politics in the 1880s as a member of the liberal reform movement. He was elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in the first Diet elections of 1890 and quickly became one of the leading voices of the opposition. He served as [[Speaker of the House of Commoners of Okaiken|Speaker of the House of Commoners]] from 1892 to 1897, where he earned a reputation as a skilled parliamentarian and a fierce advocate for the Diet's constitutional prerogatives.

In 1898, Koyanagi helped orchestrate the merger of the two major opposition parties—the Liberal Party and the Progressive Party—into the Kenseitō (Constitutional Association). This merger created the first mass-based political party in Okaiken's history, with a membership of over 100,000 and a strong base in the commercial and professional classes.

==First Premiership (1898)==
[[File:First Party Government Kenseito 1898.png|thumb|right|200px|The Kenseitō cabinet, the first party-based government in Okaiken's history]]
Koyanagi was appointed Prime Minister on 3 March 1898, following the resignation of [[Juichi Miyazaki]]'s third government. His appointment was a watershed moment: for the first time in Okaiken's history, a party politician—rather than a genrō or a bureaucrat—held the premiership.

The Kenseitō cabinet, however, was doomed from the start. The party was an uneasy coalition of two distinct political traditions: the Liberals, who represented the rural commercial interests and advocated for lower taxes and land reform, and the Progressives, who represented the urban professional classes and advocated for industrial development and expanded suffrage. These two factions had fundamentally different priorities, and their alliance was held together only by their shared opposition to the genrō-dominated government.

The government collapsed after only six months. The immediate cause was a dispute over the budget: the Progressive faction demanded increased spending on education and infrastructure, while the Liberal faction insisted on tax cuts for farmers. When Koyanagi attempted to compromise, both factions rejected his proposal, and the government lost a vote of confidence in the Diet.

Koyanagi resigned on 4 September 1898. The genrō council responded to the failure of the first party government by bringing back [[Kanezane Enoki]] for a second term, a clear signal that the establishment was not yet ready to accept party-based rule.

Despite its brevity, the first Koyanagi government achieved several notable policy outcomes. The Industrial Development Bank Act of 1898, which passed before the government's collapse, established a state-backed financial institution to provide long-term credit to emerging industries—a measure that would prove instrumental in the nation's industrial expansion. The government also introduced the first labor safety regulations in Sanese history, the Factory Safety Order of 1898, which mandated minimum safety standards in mines and factories.

==Second Premiership (1911–1913)==
Koyanagi returned to power on 12 August 1911, succeeding [[Seinosuke Matsuoka]]. By this time, the political landscape had shifted significantly. The Kenseitō had been reconstituted, and the Go-Shōkō Democracy movement was gaining momentum. Koyanagi's second government was explicitly committed to civilian control of the government and the expansion of democratic institutions.

The second Koyanagi government pursued an ambitious reform agenda. It introduced legislation to expand the franchise, strengthen labor protections, and reduce the influence of the military in politics. Koyanagi also attempted to reform the tax system to make it more progressive, and he championed the cause of universal education.

However, the government's relationship with the military was increasingly adversarial. The Imperial Sanese Army, which had been expanding its operations in the [[Shendan]] region, demanded significant increases in military spending. When Koyanagi refused to approve the Army's budget request for Shendan operations in early 1913, the military responded by withdrawing its officers from the cabinet—a move that effectively paralyzed the government.

The political crisis that followed demonstrated the fundamental weakness of civilian government under the Saito Constitution.
[[File:Koyanagi Military Crisis 1913.png|thumb|right|200px|The Army's withdrawal from the cabinet in 1913, which paralyzed the Koyanagi government]]
The Army's ability to bring down a government by withdrawing its cabinet members revealed the extent to which the military had become an independent political force, beyond the control of either the prime minister or the Diet.

Koyanagi resigned on 17 February 1913, after refusing to accede to the Army's demands. He was succeeded by [[Reijiro Masuko]], who was appointed as a compromise candidate acceptable to both the parties and the military.

In foreign policy, the second Koyanagi government sought to balance the competing demands of Shendan commercial expansion and fiscal restraint. Koyanagi authorized the renewal of the Shendan Commercial Commission's charter and negotiated a modest expansion of Sanese fishing rights in the southern Shendan, but he resisted the Army's demands for a more aggressive posture toward the Illypnian colonial powers. His government's Shendan policy was characterized by what critics called "commercial diplomacy"—the use of trade agreements and economic incentives rather than military force to advance Sanese interests in the region.

The Koyanagi government also confronted the growing Shansian territorial disputes. The Army demanded a military buildup along the northern border, but Koyanagi insisted on a diplomatic approach, negotiating a temporary fishing rights agreement with Shansi that reduced tensions along the maritime boundary. The agreement was unpopular with nationalists but prevented an escalation that the nation's fragile finances could not have sustained.

==Legacy==
Ryuichi Koyanagi is remembered as the pioneer of party government in Okaiken. His first premiership, though brief and unsuccessful, demonstrated that political parties could form a government—a concept that had been fiercely resisted by the genrō establishment. His second term's confrontation with the military anticipated the central political conflict of the Go-Shōkō era: the struggle between civilian democracy and military autonomy.

Koyanagi's failure to establish civilian control over the military had lasting consequences. The precedent set by the Army's withdrawal from his cabinet in 1913 would be invoked repeatedly in subsequent decades, as the military increasingly used its constitutional prerogatives to undermine democratic governance.

After his resignation, Koyanagi remained a member of the Diet and continued to advocate for democratic reform until his death in [[Okami]] on 15 October 1922.

